# Brickworks in Spanish (Español): the words of the interface, chosen in
# Settings, Language (src/ui/language.gd). Every word the app draws is written in
# English; each entry here is one of them, as msgid, and how it reads in this
# language, as msgstr.
#
# An entry with %s or %d in its msgid is a pattern: any words, or a number, in that
# place, put into the msgstr's own %s or %d in order ("%2$s" names the second).
# An msgid with no words of its own ("%s %d", "%s, %s") is used only where the
# words it takes have entries. A row's key after its words, a tooltip's lines,
# a line's parts joined by " · ", " — ", ". " or "; ", and "…" at a row's end
# are matched without entries of their own; so is a row's words with its first
# letter lowered, as the keys sheet writes them.
#
# The LEGO® names for parts, as the LEGO Group's own Spanish pages write them:
# ladrillo, placa, baldosa, teja, espiga, placa base, minifigura, instrucciones de montaje, paso, subconjunto. LEGO® stays an adjective. Colours keep LDraw's and
# BrickLink's names, as BrickLink Studio does in every language; keys keep the
# names the menus write them with (Ctrl, Shift, Alt, Esc, Enter).
#
# src/dev/languages_probe.gd says which words have no entry (--missing=FILE).
msgid ""
msgstr ""
"Project-Id-Version: Brickworks\n"
"Language: es\n"
"MIME-Version: 1.0\n"
"Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8\n"
"Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit\n"

